Subject: runtime: run on OS threads through WASIX with the wasix tag and
 GOWASM=threads
Last-Update: 2026-10-04

With the wasix tag and GOWASM=threads a wasip1 module runs its
goroutines on OS threads. newosproc spawns a thread with
thread_spawn_v2, which WASIX runs as another instance of the module
over the shared memory, entered at wasi_thread_start on the M's g0
stack. Go's locks sleep on WASIX futexes, each M records its WASIX
thread id as its procid, and the scheduler's wasm special cases, no
sysmon and one P among them, hold only for the single-threaded builds,
so GOMAXPROCS above one works. Asynchronous preemption stays off.

A thread that exits stores a word that lets another thread free and
reuse its g0 stack, and then its M, at once. exitThread therefore
switches to a static exit stack before the store and ends the thread
from there.

NumCPU and the default GOMAXPROCS come from WASIX's thread_parallelism,
which is what wasix-libc reports for sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN), so
a Go guest sees the count a C guest does. GOMAXPROCS in the environment
overrides it, and the count is one when the call fails.

procyield returned at once on wasm, so the spin phases of the runtime's
mutex and of sync.Mutex ran their attempts back to back and gave up
within nanoseconds, after which a waiter sleeps in a futex, a call into
WASIX. With OS threads the holder is often on another thread and about
to release, so the threaded runtime pauses for the cycles asked, one
atomic fence each, as other ports spend a PAUSE instruction. The
single-threaded builds keep returning at once.

publicationBarrier, a return on the single-threaded port, is an atomic
fence on OS threads: mallocgc needs the stores that initialize an object
ordered before the store that publishes it on a machine with weak memory
ordering, and the fence is the one barrier wasm has.

